WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.120
Good morning, Hank.

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:02.120
It's Tuesday.

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:03.120
I got a little bit of the tiny chicken disease.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.120
Hence, the husky voice.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:05.120
So, I really loved your snow goose migration
video.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.670
I thought it was fresh and smart and really
just lovely, but I can't help but notice that

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:12.730
for the first time in, like, several years,
you ended a video without saying, "John, I'll

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:13.730
see you on Tuesday."

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:17.420
Actually several nerdfighters noticed this,
and by several, I mean, these two people and

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:18.420
myself.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:21.060
Anyway, leaving off the "John, I'll see you
on Tuesday" was definitely the correct artistic

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.170
decision, but it still felt weird to me and
it made me wonder why we have a sign-off in

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:25.170
the first place.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:28.220
Like, Ze Franck’s ‘The Show’ inspired
most of the conventions of Vlogbrothers videos,

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:29.220
right.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.270
Like the jump cuts, the ‘Good morning’
etc.

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:33.739
But Ze never had a consistent sign off, so
for a while I was, like, reveling in our originality

00:00:33.739 --> 00:00:38.579
and creativity and then I remembered something
from the hazy distant past: ‘I gotta go’.

00:00:38.579 --> 00:00:42.050
Now to the vast majority of you that means
I have to go but in fact I think it will mean

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:43.050
something else to Hank.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.429
As an experiment I’m gonna call him right
now.

00:00:44.429 --> 00:00:45.710
(On phone) I’m gonna call you back in about
twenty seconds.

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:49.350
I’m gonna play you a short audio clip and
I want you to, uh, video tape yourself.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:50.350
OK, hold on.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:51.890
Audio clip: And now here’s Ian.

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:56.019
Ian: I’m one of those guys who went to school
until I couldn’t get any more degrees.

00:00:56.019 --> 00:00:59.050
Young people today could probably benefit
from my twelve years in college so here’s

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:00.050
some advice.

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:01.829
Avoid any course called Colonial American
Literature.

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:05.800
You will read smug creeps like Benjamin Franklin,
psychotics like Edgar Allan Poe, half-smart

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:09.410
mystics like Ralph Waldo Emerson and turgid
pulp writers like James Fenimore Cooper, all

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:10.410
of them dull.

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:14.430
John: What I’m hoping for is I’m hoping
you will remember the three word catchphrase

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:15.430
of that guy.

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:17.030
Hank: Is it 'I gotta go'?

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:19.060
John: YEAHHHH!!!

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:20.060
It is 'I gotta go'!

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:21.640
Hank: I don’t even know where that came
from.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:22.640
John: Perfect.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:23.640
I’ll talk to you later.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.150
Hank: It’s deep, it’s deep in my brain
John.

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:26.150
John: Alright, I gotta go.

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:27.440
I gotta make, I gotta make a video.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.840
So Ian Shoales was this public radio commentator
and when Hank and I were kids our parents

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.520
had his tape called ‘I Gotta Go’.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.530
How do I explain tapes to young people?

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:38.730
They were kinda like Spotify only not free
and it only came with twelve tracks on it

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:40.220
and it was literally made out of tape.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:41.220
It was very weird.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.740
Anyway Hank, when I was, like, fourteen and
you were, like, eleven we listened to Ian

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:47.680
Shoales constantly and we loved him because
he was funny and he talked fast and he was

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:51.960
smart without seeming snobbish or inaccessible,
and plus he never got boring because his commentaries

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:55.020
were never more than four minutes long and
they always ended with him saying ‘I gotta

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.020
go’.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:59.140
I mean Hank, we listened to that tape hundreds
of times, the fact that you remember ‘I

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:02.280
gotta go’ even though it’s been twenty
years since you last heard Ian Shoales say

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.170
those words, that says something.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:07.450
And we were clearly hugely influenced by Ian
Shoales, not just because we have a sign off,

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:12.010
but because everything he was doing in 1985
is stuff that we’re trying to do now.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:15.280
By the way Hank, it turns out that Ian Shoales
is a character created by a guy named Merle

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:18.810
Kessler who is still doing theatre and radio
work in northern California and who is still

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:19.810
very funny.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:24.310
He’s still working, and his work is still
rippling outward, and, for me at least, that’s

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.760
the definition of a successful creative life.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:30.180
In a recent episode of The Art Assignment
a viewer asked Sarah what her definition of

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:34.489
art was and she had this great answer about
being comfortable not having a definition.

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:36.909
And then I, like, blathered on for a while
without saying anything.

00:02:36.909 --> 00:02:40.849
The thing is we tend to imagine creative enterprises
as, like, singular feats of genius, right.

00:02:40.849 --> 00:02:45.969
You know, James Joyce, half-blind holed up
in an apartment writing Ulysses or Beyoncé

00:02:45.969 --> 00:02:49.420
releasing a brilliant album without assistance
or warning.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:53.610
But even if you’re James Joyce or Beyoncé,
your network of influence is vast and it stretches

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.110
back further than human memory.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:58.659
In the Unites States it’s almost a national
obligation to praise individuals but in truth

00:02:58.659 --> 00:03:02.739
I don’t think individuals really make stuff
so much as they process their influences and

00:03:02.739 --> 00:03:06.849
try to build upon them in the hopes that they
can make stuff that will be helpful to others.

00:03:06.849 --> 00:03:10.530
Contrary to the prevailing narrative, I don’t
think art is really a story of great individuals

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:12.340
trying to make their mark upon the world.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:17.049
It’s really lots of people working together
across time and space trying to make the world

00:03:17.049 --> 00:03:19.700
suck less for ourselves and for each other.

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:21.150
That’s my definition of art actually.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:23.810
You can find a link to a couple Ian Shoales
commentaries in the dooblydoo.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:26.900
Mr. Shoales, I'm a little bit embarrassed
that I’ve been making videos deeply inspired

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:31.409
by you for the past seven and a half years
without knowing it, but belatedly, thank you.

00:03:31.409 --> 00:03:32.480
Hank, I gotta go.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.480
Alright Hank, while I have this husky voice
I’m just gonna try and sing Whitney Houston’s,

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.060
uh, The Bodyguard song.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:38.060
Ready.

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:39.629
This is just the refrain.

00:03:39.629 --> 00:03:51.400
‘And I-ee-I will always love you-oooooo-ooo-oo’
(ooo barely audible and squeaky)

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:52.930
Didn’t get there.

